      <li><a href="/openshift-docs-markdown/networking/advanced_networking/ptp/configuring-ptp#cnf-configuring-fifo-priority-scheduling-for-ptp_configuring-ptp">Configuring FIFO priority scheduling for PTP hardware</a></li>
      <li><a href="/openshift-docs-markdown/networking/advanced_networking/ptp/ptp-cloud-events-consumer-dev-reference-v2#cnf-configuring-the-ptp-fast-event-publisher-v2_ptp-consumer">Configuring the PTP fast event notifications publisher</a></li>
      </ul>
      </section>
      <h3 id="nw-columbiaville-ptp-config-refererence_configuring-ptp" class="group scroll-mt-20">Intel Columbiaville E800 series NIC as PTP ordinary clock reference<a href="#nw-columbiaville-ptp-config-refererence_configuring-ptp" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h3>
      <p class="_abstract">The following table describes the changes that you must make to the reference PTP configuration to use Intel Columbiaville E800 series NICs as ordinary clocks. Make the changes in a <code>PtpConfig</code> custom resource (CR) that you apply to the cluster.</p>
      <p><strong>Recommended PTP settings for Intel Columbiaville NIC</strong></p>
      <Table class="my-4">
      <TableHeader>
      <TableRow>
      <TableHead>PTP configuration</TableHead>
      <TableHead>Recommended setting</TableHead>
      </TableRow>
      </TableHeader>
      <TableBody>
      <TableRow>
      <TableCell><code>phc2sysOpts</code></TableCell>
      <TableCell><code>-a -r -m -n 24 -N 8 -R 16</code></TableCell>
      </TableRow>
      <TableRow>
      <TableCell><code>tx_timestamp_timeout</code></TableCell>
      <TableCell><code>50</code></TableCell>
      </TableRow>
      <TableRow>
      <TableCell><code>boundary_clock_jbod</code></TableCell>
      <TableCell><code>0</code></TableCell>
      </TableRow>
      </TableBody>
      </Table>
      <Alert variant="note" class="my-4">
        <AlertTitle>Note</AlertTitle>
        <AlertDescription>
          <p>For <code>phc2sysOpts</code>, <code>-m</code> prints messages to <code>stdout</code>. The <code>linuxptp-daemon</code> <code>DaemonSet</code> parses the logs and generates Prometheus metrics.</p>
        </AlertDescription>
      </Alert>
      <h3 id="configuring-linuxptp-services-oc-dual-port_configuring-ptp" class="group scroll-mt-20">Configuring linuxptp services as an ordinary clock with dual-port NIC redundancy<a href="#configuring-linuxptp-services-oc-dual-port_configuring-ptp" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h3>
      <p class="_abstract">You can configure <code>linuxptp</code> services (<code>ptp4l</code>, <code>phc2sys</code>) as an ordinary clock with dual-port NIC redundancy by creating a <code>PtpConfig</code> custom resource (CR) object.</p>
      <p>In a dual-port NIC configuration for an ordinary clock, if one port fails, the standby port takes over, maintaining PTP timing synchronization.</p>
      <Alert variant="info" class="my-4">
        <AlertTitle>Important</AlertTitle>
        <AlertDescription>
          <p>Configuring linuxptp services as an ordinary clock with dual-port NIC redundancy is a Technology Preview feature only. Technology Preview features are not supported with Red Hat production service level agreements (SLAs) and might not be functionally complete. Red Hat does not recommend using them in production. These features provide early access to upcoming product features, enabling customers to test functionality and provide feedback during the development process.</p>
          <p>For more information about the support scope of Red Hat Technology Preview features, see <a href="https://access.redhat.com/support/offerings/techpreview/">Technology Preview Features Support Scope</a>.</p>
        </AlertDescription>
      </Alert>
      <p><strong>Prerequisites</strong></p>
      <ul>
      <li>Install the OpenShift CLI (<code>oc</code>).</li>
      <li>Log in as a user with <code>cluster-admin</code> privileges.</li>
      <li>Install the PTP Operator.</li>
      <li>Check the hardware requirements for using your dual-port NIC as an ordinary clock with added redundancy. For further information, see "Using dual-port NICs to improve redundancy for PTP ordinary clocks".</li>
      </ul>
      <p><strong>Procedure</strong></p>
      <ol>
      <li>
      Create the following <code>PtpConfig</code> CR, and then save the YAML in the <code>oc-dual-port-ptp-config.yaml</code> file.
      <CodeBlock code={"apiVersion: ptp.openshift.io/v1\nkind: PtpConfig\nmetadata:\n  name: ordinary-clock-1\n  namespace: openshift-ptp\nspec:\n  profile:\n  - name: oc-dual-port\n    phc2sysOpts: -a -r -n 24 -N 8 -R 16 -u 0\n    ptp4lConf: |-\n      [ens3f2]\n      masterOnly 0\n      [ens3f3]\n      masterOnly 0\n\n      [global]\n      #\n      # Default Data Set\n      #\n      slaveOnly 1\n#..."} lang="yaml" title="Example PTP ordinary clock dual-port configuration" />
      <p>where:</p>
      <dl class="my-4 space-y-2">
      <dt class="font-semibold"><code>phc2sysOpts: -a -r -n 24 -N 8 -R 16 -u 0</code></dt>
      <dd class="ml-6 text-muted-foreground"><p>Specifies the system config options for the <code>phc2sys</code> service.</p></dd>
      <dt class="font-semibold"><code>ptp4lConf</code></dt>
      <dd class="ml-6 text-muted-foreground"><p>Specifies the interface configuration for the <code>ptp4l</code> service. In this example, setting <code>masterOnly 0</code> for the <code>ens3f2</code> and <code>ens3f3</code> interfaces enables both ports on the <code>ens3</code> interface to run as leader or follower clocks. In combination with the <code>slaveOnly 1</code> specification, this configuration ensures one port operates as the active ordinary clock, and the other port operates as a standby ordinary clock in the <code>Listening</code> port state.</p></dd>
      <dt class="font-semibold"><code>slaveOnly 1</code></dt>
      <dd class="ml-6 text-muted-foreground"><p>Configures <code>ptp4l</code> to run as an ordinary clock only.</p></dd>
      </dl>
      </li>
      <li>
      Create the <code>PtpConfig</code> CR by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc create -f oc-dual-port-ptp-config.yaml"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      </ol>
      <p><strong>Verification</strong></p>
      <ol>
      <li>
      Check that the <code>PtpConfig</code> profile is applied to the node.
      <ol>
      <li>
      Get the list of pods in the <code>openshift-ptp</code> namespace by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc get pods -n openshift-ptp -o wide"} lang="terminal" />
      <CodeBlock code={"NAME                            READY   STATUS    RESTARTS   AGE   IP               NODE\nlinuxptp-daemon-4xkbb           1/1     Running   0          43m   10.1.196.24      compute-0.example.com\nlinuxptp-daemon-tdspf           1/1     Running   0          43m   10.1.196.25      compute-1.example.com\nptp-operator-657bbb64c8-2f8sj   1/1     Running   0          43m   10.129.0.61      control-plane-1.example.com"} lang="terminal" title="Example output" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Check that the profile is correct. Examine the logs of the <code>linuxptp</code> daemon that corresponds to the node you specified in the <code>PtpConfig</code> profile. Run the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc logs linuxptp-daemon-4xkbb -n openshift-ptp -c linuxptp-daemon-container"} lang="terminal" />
      <CodeBlock code={"I1115 09:41:17.117596 4143292 daemon.go:107] in applyNodePTPProfile\nI1115 09:41:17.117604 4143292 daemon.go:109] updating NodePTPProfile to:\nI1115 09:41:17.117607 4143292 daemon.go:110] ------------------------------------\nI1115 09:41:17.117612 4143292 daemon.go:102] Profile Name: oc-dual-port\nI1115 09:41:17.117616 4143292 daemon.go:102] Interface: ens787f1\nI1115 09:41:17.117620 4143292 daemon.go:102] Ptp4lOpts: -2 --summary_interval -4\nI1115 09:41:17.117623 4143292 daemon.go:102] Phc2sysOpts: -a -r -n 24 -N 8 -R 16 -u 0\nI1115 09:41:17.117626 4143292 daemon.go:116] ------------------------------------"} lang="terminal" title="Example output" />
      </li>
      </ol>
      </li>
      </ol>
      <section class="related-resources _additional-resources">
      <p class="related-resources-title">Additional resources</p>
      <ul>
      <li><a href="/openshift-docs-markdown/networking/advanced_networking/ptp/configuring-ptp#configuring-linuxptp-services-as-ordinary-clock_configuring-ptp">Configuring linuxptp services as ordinary clock</a></li>
      <li><a href="/openshift-docs-markdown/networking/advanced_networking/ptp/about-ptp#ptp-dual-ports-oc_about-ptp">Using dual-port NICs to improve redundancy for PTP ordinary clocks</a></li>
      </ul>
      </section>
      <h2 id="cnf-configuring-fifo-priority-scheduling-for-ptp_configuring-ptp" class="group scroll-mt-20">Configuring FIFO priority scheduling for PTP hardware<a href="#cnf-configuring-fifo-priority-scheduling-for-ptp_configuring-ptp" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h2>
      <p class="_abstract">In telco or other deployment types that require low latency performance, PTP daemon threads run in a constrained CPU footprint alongside the rest of the infrastructure components. By default, PTP threads run with the <code>SCHED_OTHER</code> policy. Under high load, these threads might not get the scheduling latency they require for error-free operation.</p>
      <p>To mitigate against potential scheduling latency errors, you can configure the PTP Operator <code>linuxptp</code> services to allow threads to run with a <code>SCHED_FIFO</code> policy. If <code>SCHED_FIFO</code> is set for a <code>PtpConfig</code> CR, then <code>ptp4l</code> and <code>phc2sys</code> will run in the parent container under <code>chrt</code> with a priority set by the <code>ptpSchedulingPriority</code> field of the <code>PtpConfig</code> CR.</p>
      <Alert variant="note" class="my-4">
        <AlertTitle>Note</AlertTitle>
        <AlertDescription>
          <p>Setting <code>ptpSchedulingPolicy</code> is optional, and is only required if you are experiencing latency errors.</p>
        </AlertDescription>
      </Alert>
      <p><strong>Procedure</strong></p>
      <ol>
      <li>
      Edit the <code>PtpConfig</code> CR profile:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc edit PtpConfig -n openshift-ptp"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Change the <code>ptpSchedulingPolicy</code> and <code>ptpSchedulingPriority</code> fields:
      <CodeBlock code={"apiVersion: ptp.openshift.io/v1\nkind: PtpConfig\nmetadata:\n  name: <ptp_config_name>\n  namespace: openshift-ptp\n...\nspec:\n  profile:\n  - name: \"profile1\"\n...\n    ptpSchedulingPolicy: SCHED_FIFO\n    ptpSchedulingPriority: 10"} lang="yaml" />
      <p>where:</p>
      <dl class="my-4 space-y-2">
      <dt class="font-semibold"><code>ptpSchedulingPolicy: SCHED_FIFO</code></dt>
      <dd class="ml-6 text-muted-foreground"><p>Sets the scheduling policy for <code>ptp4l</code> and <code>phc2sys</code> processes. Use <code>SCHED_FIFO</code> on systems that support FIFO scheduling.</p></dd>
      <dt class="font-semibold"><code>ptpSchedulingPriority: 10</code></dt>
      <dd class="ml-6 text-muted-foreground"><p>Sets the integer value 1-65 used to configure FIFO priority for <code>ptp4l</code> and <code>phc2sys</code> processes.</p></dd>
      </dl>
      </li>
      <li>Save and exit to apply the changes to the <code>PtpConfig</code> CR.</li>
      </ol>
      <p><strong>Verification</strong></p>
      <ol>
      <li>
      Get the name of the <code>linuxptp-daemon</code> pod and corresponding node where the <code>PtpConfig</code> CR has been applied:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc get pods -n openshift-ptp -o wide"} lang="terminal" />
      <CodeBlock code={"NAME                            READY   STATUS    RESTARTS   AGE     IP            NODE\nlinuxptp-daemon-gmv2n           3/3     Running   0          1d17h   10.1.196.24   compute-0.example.com\nlinuxptp-daemon-lgm55           3/3     Running   0          1d17h   10.1.196.25   compute-1.example.com\nptp-operator-3r4dcvf7f4-zndk7   1/1     Running   0          1d7h    10.129.0.61   control-plane-1.example.com"} lang="terminal" title="Example output" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Check that the <code>ptp4l</code> process is running with the updated <code>chrt</code> FIFO priority:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc -n openshift-ptp logs linuxptp-daemon-lgm55 -c linuxptp-daemon-container|grep chrt"} lang="terminal" />
      <CodeBlock code={"I1216 19:24:57.091872 1600715 daemon.go:285] /bin/chrt -f 65 /usr/sbin/ptp4l -f /var/run/ptp4l.0.config -2  --summary_interval -4 -m"} lang="terminal" title="Example output" />
      </li>
      </ol>
      <h2 id="cnf-configuring-log-reduction-for-linuxptp_configuring-ptp" class="group scroll-mt-20">Configuring PTP log reduction<a href="#cnf-configuring-log-reduction-for-linuxptp_configuring-ptp" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h2>
      <p class="_abstract">The <code>linuxptp-daemon</code> generates logs that you can use for debugging purposes. In telco or other deployment types that feature a limited storage capacity, these logs can add to the storage demand. Currently, the default logging rate is high, causing logs to rotate out in under 24 hours, which makes it difficult to track changes and identify problems.</p>
      <p>You can achieve basic log reduction by configuring the <code>PtpConfig</code> custom resource (CR) to exclude log messages that report the master offset value. The master offset log message reports the difference between the clock of the current node and the master clock in nanoseconds. However, with this method, there is no summary status of filtered logs. The enhanced log reduction feature allows you to configure the logging rate of PTP logs. You can set a specific logging rate, which can help reduce the volume of logs generated by the <code>linuxptp-daemon</code> while still retaining essential information for troubleshooting. With the enhanced log reduction feature, you can also specify a threshold that still displays the offset logs if the offset is higher than that threshold.</p>
      <h3 id="cnf-configuring-log-filtering-for-linuxptp_configuring-ptp" class="group scroll-mt-20">Configuring log filtering for PTP<a href="#cnf-configuring-log-filtering-for-linuxptp_configuring-ptp" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h3>
      <p class="_abstract">Modify the <code>PtpConfig</code> custom resource (CR) to configure basic log filtering and exclude log messages that report the master offset value.</p>
      <p><strong>Prerequisites</strong></p>
      <ul>
      <li>Install the OpenShift CLI (<code>oc</code>).</li>
      <li>Log in as a user with <code>cluster-admin</code> privileges.</li>
      <li>Install the PTP Operator.</li>
      </ul>
      <p><strong>Procedure</strong></p>
      <ol>
      <li>
      Edit the <code>PtpConfig</code> CR:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc edit PtpConfig -n openshift-ptp"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      In <code>spec.profile</code>, add the <code>ptpSettings.logReduce</code> specification and set the value to <code>true</code>:
      <CodeBlock code={"apiVersion: ptp.openshift.io/v1\nkind: PtpConfig\nmetadata:\n  name: <ptp_config_name>\n  namespace: openshift-ptp\n...\nspec:\n  profile:\n  - name: \"profile1\"\n...\n    ptpSettings:\n      logReduce: \"true\""} lang="yaml" />
      <Alert variant="note" class="my-4">
        <AlertTitle>Note</AlertTitle>
        <AlertDescription>
          <p>For debugging purposes, you can revert this specification to <code>False</code> to include the master offset messages.</p>
        </AlertDescription>
      </Alert>
      </li>
      <li>Save and exit to apply the changes to the <code>PtpConfig</code> CR.</li>
      </ol>
      <p><strong>Verification</strong></p>
      <ol>
      <li>
      Get the name of the <code>linuxptp-daemon</code> pod and corresponding node where the <code>PtpConfig</code> CR has been applied:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc get pods -n openshift-ptp -o wide"} lang="terminal" />
      <CodeBlock code={"NAME                            READY   STATUS    RESTARTS   AGE     IP            NODE\nlinuxptp-daemon-gmv2n           3/3     Running   0          1d17h   10.1.196.24   compute-0.example.com\nlinuxptp-daemon-lgm55           3/3     Running   0          1d17h   10.1.196.25   compute-1.example.com\nptp-operator-3r4dcvf7f4-zndk7   1/1     Running   0          1d7h    10.129.0.61   control-plane-1.example.com"} lang="terminal" title="Example output" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Verify that master offset messages are excluded from the logs by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc -n openshift-ptp logs <linux_daemon_container> -c linuxptp-daemon-container | grep \"master offset\""} lang="terminal" />
      <ul>
      <li>
      <code>&lt;linux_daemon_container&gt;</code> is the name of the <code>linuxptp-daemon</code> pod, for example <code>linuxptp-daemon-gmv2n</code>.
      <p>When you configure the <code>logReduce</code> specification, this command does not report any instances of <code>master offset</code> in the logs of the <code>linuxptp</code> daemon.</p>
      </li>
      </ul>
      </li>
      </ol>
      <h3 id="cnf-configuring-enhanced-log-reduction-for-linuxptp_configuring-ptp" class="group scroll-mt-20">Configuring enhanced PTP log reduction<a href="#cnf-configuring-enhanced-log-reduction-for-linuxptp_configuring-ptp" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h3>
      <p class="_abstract">Basic log reduction effectively filters out frequent logs. However, if you want a periodic summary of the filtered logs, use the enhanced log reduction feature.</p>
      <p><strong>Prerequisites</strong></p>
      <ul>
      <li>Install the OpenShift CLI (<code>oc</code>).</li>
      <li>Log in as a user with <code>cluster-admin</code> privileges.</li>
      <li>Install the PTP Operator.</li>
      </ul>
      <p><strong>Procedure</strong></p>
      <ol>
      <li>
      Edit the <code>PtpConfig</code> custom resource (CR):
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc edit PtpConfig -n openshift-ptp"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Add the <code>ptpSettings.logReduce</code> specification in the <code>spec.profile</code> section, and set the value to <code>enhanced</code>:
      <CodeBlock code={"apiVersion: ptp.openshift.io/v1\nkind: PtpConfig\nmetadata:\n  name: <ptp_config_name>\n  namespace: openshift-ptp\n...\nspec:\n  profile:\n  - name: \"profile1\"\n...\n    ptpSettings:\n      logReduce: \"enhanced\""} lang="yaml" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Optional: Configure the interval for summary logs and a threshold in nanoseconds for the master offset logs. For example, to set the interval to 60 seconds and the threshold to 100 nanoseconds, add the <code>ptpSettings.logReduce</code> specification in the <code>spec.profile</code> section and set the value to <code>enhanced 60s 100</code>.
      <CodeBlock code={"apiVersion: ptp.openshift.io/v1\nkind: PtpConfig\nmetadata:\n  name: <ptp_config_name>\n  namespace: openshift-ptp\nspec:\n  profile:\n  - name: \"profile1\"\n    ptpSettings:\n      logReduce: \"enhanced 60s 100\""} lang="yaml" />
      <ul><li>By default, the <code>linuxptp-daemon</code> is configured to generate summary logs every 30 seconds if no value is specified. In the example configuration, the daemon generates summary logs every 60 seconds and a threshold of 100 nanoseconds for the master offset logs is set. This means the daemon only produces summary logs at the specified interval. However, if your clock’s offset from the master exceeds plus or minus 100 nanoseconds, that specific log entry is recorded.</li></ul>
      </li>
      <li>
      Optional: To set the interval without a master offset threshold, configure the <code>logReduce</code> field to <code>enhanced 60s</code> in the YAML.
      <CodeBlock code={"apiVersion: ptp.openshift.io/v1\nkind: PtpConfig\nmetadata:\n  name: <ptp_config_name>\n  namespace: openshift-ptp\nspec:\n  profile:\n  - name: \"profile1\"\n    ptpSettings:\n      logReduce: \"enhanced 60s\""} lang="yaml" />
      </li>
      <li>Save and exit to apply the changes to the <code>PtpConfig</code> CR.</li>
      </ol>
      <p><strong>Verification</strong></p>
      <ol>
      <li>
      Get the name of the <code>linuxptp-daemon</code> pod and the corresponding node where the <code>PtpConfig</code> CR is applied by running the following command
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc get pods -n openshift-ptp -o wide"} lang="terminal" />
      <CodeBlock code={"NAME                            READY   STATUS    RESTARTS   AGE     IP            NODE\nlinuxptp-daemon-gmv2n           3/3     Running   0          1d17h   10.1.196.24   compute-0.example.com\nlinuxptp-daemon-lgm55           3/3     Running   0          1d17h   10.1.196.25   compute-1.example.com\nptp-operator-3r4dcvf7f4-zndk7   1/1     Running   0          1d7h    10.129.0.61   control-plane-1.example.com"} lang="terminal" title="Example output" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Verify that master offset messages are excluded from the logs by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc -n openshift-ptp logs <linux_daemon_container> -c linuxptp-daemon-container | grep \"master offset\""} lang="terminal" />
      <ul><li><code>&lt;linux_daemon_container&gt;</code> is the name of the <code>linuxptp-daemon</code> pod, for example, <code>linuxptp-daemon-gmv2n</code>.</li></ul>
      </li>
      </ol>
      <h2 id="cnf-configuring-gnss-ntp-failover_configuring-ptp" class="group scroll-mt-20">Configuring GNSS failover to NTP for time synchronization continuity<a href="#cnf-configuring-gnss-ntp-failover_configuring-ptp" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h2>
      <p class="_abstract">Automatic failover from global navigation satellite system (GNSS) to Network Time Protocol (NTP) maintains time synchronization continuity when the primary signal is lost, ensuring system stability for telco operations.</p>
      <p>Telco operators require time source redundancy to ensure time synchronization continuity and system stability.</p>
      <p>OpenShift Container Platform provides automatic failover capabilities to maintain synchronization. The system utilizes GNSS (delivered by <code>phc2sys</code>) as the primary time source. To protect against primary signal loss, such as jamming or antenna failure, the system automatically transitions to the secondary time source, NTP delivered by <code>chronyd</code>. Upon signal recovery, the system automatically switches back to and resumes synchronization with <code>phc2sys</code>.</p>
      <p>You can control the resilience of the time synchronization by setting the <code>ts2phc.holdover</code> parameter in seconds. This value dictates the maximum time the internal control algorithm can continue synchronizing the PHC after the main time of day (ToD) source such as a GNSS receiver is lost. The algorithm can only continue if it remains in a stable state (SERVO_LOCKED_STABLE). When the process exits this configured holdover period, it signifies an unrecoverable primary signal loss. The system then allows failover to a secondary source such as NTP.</p>
      <h3 id="configuring-gnss-to-ntp-failover_configuring-ptp" class="group scroll-mt-20">Creating a PTP Grandmaster configuration with GNSS failover<a href="#configuring-gnss-to-ntp-failover_configuring-ptp" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h3>
      <p class="_abstract">Configure a Precision Time Protocol (PTP) Telecom Grandmaster clock with automatic failover from global navigation satellite system (GNSS) to Network Time Protocol (NTP) when satellite signals are unavailable.</p>
      <p>This procedure configures a T-GM (Telecom Grandmaster) clock that uses an Intel E810 Westport Channel NIC as the PTP grandmaster clock with GNSS to NTP failover capabilities.</p>
      <p><strong>Prerequisites</strong></p>
      <ul>
      <li>For T-GM clocks in production environments, install an Intel E810 Westport Channel NIC in the bare-metal cluster host.</li>
      <li>Install the OpenShift CLI (<code>oc</code>).</li>
      <li>Log in as a user with <code>cluster-admin</code> privileges.</li>
      <li>Install the PTP Operator.</li>
      </ul>
      <p><strong>Procedure</strong></p>
      <ol>
      <li>
      Verify the PTP Operator installation by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc get pods -n openshift-ptp -o wide"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>The output is similar to the following listing the PTP Operator pod and the <code>linuxptp-daemon</code> pods:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"NAME                            READY   STATUS    RESTARTS   AGE   IP              NODE                       NOMINATED NODE   READINESS GATES\nlinuxptp-daemon-4xk9m           2/2     Running   0          15m   192.168.1.101   worker-0.cluster.local     <none>           <none>\nlinuxptp-daemon-7bv2n           2/2     Running   0          15m   192.168.1.102   worker-1.cluster.local     <none>           <none>\nlinuxptp-daemon-9cp4r           2/2     Running   0          15m   192.168.1.103   worker-2.cluster.local     <none>           <none>\nlinuxptp-daemon-kw8h5           2/2     Running   0          15m   192.168.1.104   worker-3.cluster.local     <none>           <none>\nlinuxptp-daemon-m3j7t           2/2     Running   0          15m   192.168.1.105   worker-4.cluster.local     <none>           <none>\nptp-operator-75c77dbf86-xm9kl   1/1     Running   0          20m   10.129.0.45     master-1.cluster.local     <none>           <none>"} lang="terminal" />
      <ul>
      <li><code>ptp-operator-*</code>: The PTP Operator pod (one instance in the cluster)</li>
      <li>
      <code>linuxptp-daemon-*</code>: The linuxptp daemon pods. A daemon pod runs on each node that matches the PtpConfig profile. Each daemon pod should show <code>2/2</code> in the READY column, indicating both containers (<code>linuxptp-daemon-container</code> and <code>kube-rbac-proxy</code>) are running.
      <Alert variant="note" class="my-4">
        <AlertTitle>Note</AlertTitle>
        <AlertDescription>
          <p>The number of <code>linuxptp-daemon</code> pods is determined by the node labels defined in the <code>PtpOperatorConfig</code> which controls the DaemonSet deployment. The PtpConfig profile matching, as shown in Step 4, only determines which specific PTP settings are applied on the running daemons. In this example, the operator configuration targets all 5 worker nodes. For single-node OpenShift clusters, you will see only one <code>linuxptp-daemon</code> pod, as the configuration targets only the control plane node which acts as the worker.</p>
        </AlertDescription>
      </Alert>
      </li>
      </ul>
      </li>
      <li>
      Check which network interfaces support hardware timestamping by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc get NodePtpDevice -n openshift-ptp -o yaml"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>The output is similar to the following showing NodePtpDevice resources for nodes with PTP-capable network interfaces:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"apiVersion: v1\nitems:\n- apiVersion: ptp.openshift.io/v1\n  kind: NodePtpDevice\n  metadata:\n    name: worker-0.cluster.local\n    namespace: openshift-ptp\n  spec: {}\n  status:\n    devices:\n    - name: ens7f0\n      hwConfig:\n        phcIndex: 0\n    - name: ens7f1\n      hwConfig:\n        phcIndex: 1\n- apiVersion: ptp.openshift.io/v1\n  kind: NodePtpDevice\n  metadata:\n    name: worker-1.cluster.local\n    namespace: openshift-ptp\n  spec: {}\n  status:\n    devices:\n    - name: ens7f0\n      hwConfig:\n        phcIndex: 0\n    - name: ens7f1\n      hwConfig:\n        phcIndex: 1\nkind: List\nmetadata:\n  resourceVersion: \"\""} lang="yaml" />
      <p>In this example output:</p>
      <ul>
      <li><code>ens7f0</code> and <code>ens7f1</code> are PTP-capable interfaces (Intel E810 NIC ports).</li>
      <li>
      <code>phcIndex</code> indicates the PTP Hardware Clock number (maps to <code>/dev/ptp0</code>, <code>/dev/ptp1</code>, and so on).
      <Alert variant="note" class="my-4">
        <AlertTitle>Note</AlertTitle>
        <AlertDescription>
          <p>The output shows one NodePtpDevice resource for each node with PTP-capable interfaces. In this example, five worker nodes have Intel E810 NICs. For single-node OpenShift clusters, you would see only one NodePtpDevice resource.</p>
        </AlertDescription>
      </Alert>
      </li>
      </ul>
      </li>
      <li>
      The PTP profile uses node labels for matching. Check your machine config pool (MCP) to find the node labels by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc get mcp"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>The output is similar to the following:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"NAME     CONFIG                   UPDATED   UPDATING   DEGRADED   MACHINECOUNT   READYMACHINECOUNT   UPDATEDMACHINECOUNT DEGRADEDMACHINECOUNT AGE\nmaster   rendered-master-a1b1**   True      False      False      3              3                   3                   0                    45d\nworker   rendered-worker-f6e5**   True      False      False      5              5                   5                   0                    45d"} lang="terminal" />
      <Alert variant="note" class="my-4">
        <AlertTitle>Note</AlertTitle>
        <AlertDescription>
          <p>The CONFIG column shows a truncated hash of the rendered MachineConfig. In actual output, this will be a full 64-character hash such as <code>rendered-master-a1b2c3d4e5f6a7b8c9d0e1f2a3b4c5d6</code>.</p>
        </AlertDescription>
      </Alert>
      <ul>
      <li>In this example, the <code>&lt;MCP-name&gt;</code> is <code>worker</code> for worker nodes and <code>master</code> for control plane nodes. Most T-GM deployments use worker nodes, so you would use <code>worker</code> as the <code>&lt;MCP-name&gt;</code>.</li>
      <li>For single-node OpenShift clusters, the <code>&lt;MCP-name&gt;</code> is <code>master</code> (the worker MCP will show <code>MACHINECOUNT</code> of 0).</li>
      </ul>
      </li>
      <li>
      Create a <code>PtpConfig</code> custom resource (CR) that configures the T-GM clock with GNSS to NTP failover. Save the following YAML configuration to a file named <code>ptp-config-gnss-ntp-failover.yaml</code>, replacing <code>&lt;MCP-name&gt;</code> with the name of your machine config pool from the previous step.
      <CodeBlock code={"# The grandmaster profile is provided for testing only\n# It is not installed on production clusters\napiVersion: ptp.openshift.io/v1\nkind: PtpConfig\nmetadata:\n  name: grandmaster\n  namespace: openshift-ptp\n  annotations:\n    ran.openshift.io/ztp-deploy-wave: \"10\"\nspec:\n  profile:\n  - name: \"grandmaster\"\n    ptp4lOpts: \"-2 --summary_interval -4\"\n    phc2sysOpts: -r -u 0 -m -N 8 -R 16 -s ens7f0 -n 24\n    ptpSchedulingPolicy: SCHED_FIFO\n    ptpSchedulingPriority: 10\n    ptpSettings:\n      logReduce: \"true\"\n\n    # --- FAILOVER CONFIGURATION ---\n    # Holdover time: 14400 seconds (4 hours) before switching to NTP\n    ts2phcOpts: \"--ts2phc.holdover 14400\"\n\n    # Configure Chronyd (Secondary Time Source)\n    chronydOpts: \"-d\"\n    chronydConf: |\n      server time.nist.gov iburst\n      makestep 1.0 -1\n      pidfile /var/run/chronyd.pid\n\n    plugins:\n      # E810 Hardware-Specific Configuration\n      e810:\n        enableDefaultConfig: false\n        settings:\n          LocalHoldoverTimeout: 14400\n          LocalMaxHoldoverOffSet: 1500\n          MaxInSpecOffset: 1500\n        pins:\n          # Syntax guide:\n          # - The 1st number in each pair must be one of:\n          #    0 - Disabled\n          #    1 - RX\n          #    2 - TX\n          # - The 2nd number in each pair must match the channel number\n          ens7f0:\n            SMA1: 0 1\n            SMA2: 0 2\n            U.FL1: 0 1\n            U.FL2: 0 2\n        ublxCmds:\n          - args: #ubxtool -P 29.20 -z CFG-HW-ANT_CFG_VOLTCTRL,1\n              - \"-P\"\n              - \"29.20\"\n              - \"-z\"\n              - \"CFG-HW-ANT_CFG_VOLTCTRL,1\"\n            reportOutput: false\n          - args: #ubxtool -P 29.20 -e GPS\n              - \"-P\"\n              - \"29.20\"\n              - \"-e\"\n              - \"GPS\"\n            reportOutput: false\n          - args: #ubxtool -P 29.20 -d Galileo\n              - \"-P\"\n              - \"29.20\"\n              - \"-d\"\n              - \"Galileo\"\n            reportOutput: false\n          - args: #ubxtool -P 29.20 -d GLONASS\n              - \"-P\"\n              - \"29.20\"\n              - \"-d\"\n              - \"GLONASS\"\n            reportOutput: false\n          - args: #ubxtool -P 29.20 -d BeiDou\n              - \"-P\"\n              - \"29.20\"\n              - \"-d\"\n              - \"BeiDou\"\n            reportOutput: false\n          - args: #ubxtool -P 29.20 -d SBAS\n              - \"-P\"\n              - \"29.20\"\n              - \"-d\"\n              - \"SBAS\"\n            reportOutput: false\n          - args: #ubxtool -P 29.20 -t -w 5 -v 1 -e SURVEYIN,600,50000\n              - \"-P\"\n              - \"29.20\"\n              - \"-t\"\n              - \"-w\"\n              - \"5\"\n              - \"-v\"\n              - \"1\"\n              - \"-e\"\n              - \"SURVEYIN,600,50000\"\n            reportOutput: true\n          - args: #ubxtool -P 29.20 -p MON-HW\n              - \"-P\"\n              - \"29.20\"\n              - \"-p\"\n              - \"MON-HW\"\n            reportOutput: true\n          - args: #ubxtool -P 29.20 -p CFG-MSG,1,38,248\n              - \"-P\"\n              - \"29.20\"\n              - \"-p\"\n              - \"CFG-MSG,1,38,248\"\n            reportOutput: true\n\n      # NTP Failover Plugin\n      ntpfailover:\n        gnssFailover: true\n\n    # --- GNSS (ts2phc) CONFIGURATION (Primary Source) ---\n    ts2phcConf: |\n      [nmea]\n      ts2phc.master 1\n      [global]\n      use_syslog  0\n      verbose 1\n      logging_level 7\n      ts2phc.pulsewidth 100000000\n      ts2phc.nmea_serialport /dev/ttyGNSS_1700_0\n      leapfile  /usr/share/zoneinfo/leap-seconds.list\n      [ens7f0]\n      ts2phc.extts_polarity rising\n      ts2phc.extts_correction 0\n\n    # --- PTP4L CONFIGURATION (Grandmaster Role) ---\n    ptp4lConf: |\n      [ens7f0]\n      masterOnly 1\n      [ens7f1]\n      masterOnly 1\n      [global]\n      #\n      # Default Data Set\n      #\n      twoStepFlag 1\n      priority1 128\n      priority2 128\n      domainNumber 24\n      #utc_offset 37\n      clockClass 6\n      clockAccuracy 0x27\n      offsetScaledLogVariance 0xFFFF\n      free_running 0\n      freq_est_interval 1\n      dscp_event 0\n      dscp_general 0\n      dataset_comparison G.8275.x\n      G.8275.defaultDS.localPriority 128\n      #\n      # Port Data Set\n      #\n      logAnnounceInterval -3\n      logSyncInterval -4\n      logMinDelayReqInterval -4\n      logMinPdelayReqInterval 0\n      announceReceiptTimeout 3\n      syncReceiptTimeout 0\n      delayAsymmetry 0\n      fault_reset_interval -4\n      neighborPropDelayThresh 20000000\n      masterOnly 0\n      G.8275.portDS.localPriority 128\n      #\n      # Run time options\n      #\n      assume_two_step 0\n      logging_level 6\n      path_trace_enabled 0\n      follow_up_info 0\n      hybrid_e2e 0\n      inhibit_multicast_service 0\n      net_sync_monitor 0\n      tc_spanning_tree 0\n      tx_timestamp_timeout 50\n      unicast_listen 0\n      unicast_master_table 0\n      unicast_req_duration 3600\n      use_syslog 1\n      verbose 0\n      summary_interval -4\n      kernel_leap 1\n      check_fup_sync 0\n      clock_class_threshold 7\n      #\n      # Servo Options\n      #\n      pi_proportional_const 0.0\n      pi_integral_const 0.0\n      pi_proportional_scale 0.0\n      pi_proportional_exponent -0.3\n      pi_proportional_norm_max 0.7\n      pi_integral_scale 0.0\n      pi_integral_exponent 0.4\n      pi_integral_norm_max 0.3\n      step_threshold 2.0\n      first_step_threshold 0.00002\n      clock_servo pi\n      sanity_freq_limit  200000000\n      ntpshm_segment 0\n      #\n      # Transport options\n      #\n      transportSpecific 0x0\n      ptp_dst_mac 01:1B:19:00:00:00\n      p2p_dst_mac 01:80:C2:00:00:0E\n      udp_ttl 1\n      udp6_scope 0x0E\n      uds_address /var/run/ptp4l\n      #\n      # Default interface options\n      #\n      clock_type BC\n      network_transport L2\n      delay_mechanism E2E\n      time_stamping hardware\n      tsproc_mode filter\n      delay_filter moving_median\n      delay_filter_length 10\n      egressLatency 0\n      ingressLatency 0\n      boundary_clock_jbod 0\n      #\n      # Clock description\n      #\n      productDescription ;;\n      revisionData ;;\n      manufacturerIdentity 00:00:00\n      userDescription ;\n      timeSource 0x20\n    ptpClockThreshold:\n      holdOverTimeout: 5\n      maxOffsetThreshold: 100\n      minOffsetThreshold: -100\n  recommend:\n  - profile: \"grandmaster\"\n    priority: 4\n    match:\n    - nodeLabel: node-role.kubernetes.io/<MCP-name>"} lang="yaml" />
      <Alert variant="info" class="my-4">
        <AlertTitle>Important</AlertTitle>
        <AlertDescription>
          <p>Replace the example interface names (<code>ens7f0</code>, <code>ens7f1</code>) with your actual E810 NIC interface names found in step 2. Common E810 interface naming patterns include <code>ens7f0</code>, <code>ens8f0</code>, <code>eth0</code>, <code>enp2s0f0</code>, and so on. The exact name depends on your system firmware settings and Linux network device naming conventions. Also replace <code>/dev/ttyGNSS_1700_0</code> with your actual GNSS serial port device path. For single-node OpenShift clusters, replace <code>&lt;MCP-name&gt;</code> with <code>master</code> in the nodeLabel match. For multi-node clusters using worker nodes as T-GM, use <code>worker</code>.</p>
        </AlertDescription>
      </Alert>
      <p>The configuration includes the following components:</p>
      <ul>
      <li>
      <strong>PTP4L options:</strong>
      <ul>
      <li><code>-2</code>: Use PTP version 2</li>
      <li><code>--summary_interval -4</code>: Log summary every 2^(-4) = 0.0625 seconds</li>
      </ul>
      </li>
      <li>
      <strong>PHC2SYS options:</strong>
      <ul>
      <li><code>-r</code>: Synchronize system clock from PTP hardware clock</li>
      <li><code>-u 0</code>: Update rate multiplier</li>
      <li><code>-m</code>: Print messages to stdout</li>
      <li><code>-N 8</code>: Domain number for ptp4l</li>
      <li><code>-R 16</code>: Update rate</li>
      <li><code>-s ens7f0</code>: Source interface (replace with your E810 interface name)</li>
      <li><code>-n 24</code>: Domain number</li>
      </ul>
      </li>
      <li>
      <strong>Failover configuration:</strong>
      <ul>
      <li><code>ts2phcOpts --ts2phc.holdover 14400</code>: 4-hour holdover before switching to NTP</li>
      <li><code>chronydConf</code>: NTP server configuration for failover replace <code>time.nist.gov</code> with your preferred NTP server</li>
      <li><code>ntpfailover plugin</code>: Enables automatic GNSS-to-NTP switching with <code>gnssFailover: true</code></li>
      </ul>
      </li>
      <li>
      <strong>E810 plugin configuration:</strong>
      <ul>
      <li><code>LocalHoldoverTimeout: 14400</code>: E810 hardware holdover timeout (4 hours)</li>
      <li><code>pins</code>: Configuration for 1PPS input on E810 physical pins (U.FL2, SMA1, SMA2, U.FL1)</li>
      <li><code>ublxCmds</code>: Commands to configure u-blox GNSS receiver (enable GPS, disable other constellations, set survey-in mode)</li>
      </ul>
      </li>
      <li>
      <strong>GNSS (ts2phc) configuration:</strong>
      <ul>
      <li><code>ts2phc.nmea_serialport /dev/ttyGNSS_1700_0</code>: GNSS serial port device path (replace with your actual GNSS device)</li>
      <li><code>ts2phc.extts_polarity rising</code>: 1PPS signal on rising edge</li>
      <li><code>ts2phc.pulsewidth 100000000</code>: 1PPS pulse width in nanoseconds</li>
      </ul>
      </li>
      <li>
      <strong>PTP4L configuration:</strong>
      <ul>
      <li><code>masterOnly 1</code>: Interface acts only as PTP master</li>
      <li><code>clockClass 6</code>: GPS-synchronized quality level</li>
      <li><code>domainNumber 24</code>: PTP domain</li>
      <li><code>clock_type BC</code>: Boundary Clock mode</li>
      <li><code>time_stamping hardware</code>: Use hardware timestamps from E810 NIC</li>
      </ul>
      </li>
      </ul>
      </li>
      <li>
      Apply the <code>PtpConfig</code> CR by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc apply -f ptp-config-gnss-ntp-failover.yaml"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>The output is similar to the following:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"ptpconfig.ptp.openshift.io/grandmaster created"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      </ol>
      <p><strong>Verification</strong></p>
      <ol>
      <li>
      The PTP daemon checks for profile updates every 30 seconds. Wait approximately 30 seconds, then verify by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc get ptpconfig -n openshift-ptp"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>The output is similar to the following:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"NAME           AGE\ngrandmaster    2m"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Check the NodePtpDevice to see if the profile is applied by running the following command, replacing <code>&lt;node_name&gt;</code> with your node hostname:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc describe nodeptpdevice <node_name> -n openshift-ptp"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>For example, on a multi-node cluster with worker nodes: <code>worker-0.cluster.local</code></p>
      <p>For single-node OpenShift clusters, use the control plane node name, which you can find by running:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc get nodes"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Check if the profile is being loaded by monitoring the daemon logs:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc get pods -n openshift-ptp | grep linuxptp-daemon"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>Then check the logs, replacing <code>&lt;linuxptp-daemon-pod&gt;</code> with the actual pod name from the previous command:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc logs -n openshift-ptp <linuxptp-daemon-pod> -c linuxptp-daemon-container --tail=100"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>Success indicators in the logs are:</p>
      <ul>
      <li><code>load profiles</code> - Profile is being loaded</li>
      <li><code>in applyNodePTPProfiles</code> - Profile is being applied</li>
      <li>No <code>ptp profile doesn't exist for node</code> errors</li>
      </ul>
      </li>
      <li>
      Check <code>chronyd</code> status to verify NTP is running as the secondary time source by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc logs -n openshift-ptp <linuxptp-daemon-pod> -c linuxptp-daemon-container | grep chronyd"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>The output is similar to the following:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"chronyd version 4.5 starting\nAdded source ID#0000000001 (time.nist.gov)"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Check GNSS/gpsd by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc logs -n openshift-ptp <linuxptp-daemon-pod> -c linuxptp-daemon-container | grep gpsd"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>The output shows the following when GNSS is functioning correctly:</p>
      <ul>
      <li><code>gpsd</code> starting successfully</li>
      <li>No <code>No such file or directory</code> errors exist</li>
      </ul>
      </li>
      <li>
      Check <code>ts2phc</code> (GNSS synchronization) status by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc logs -n openshift-ptp <linuxptp-daemon-pod> -c linuxptp-daemon-container | grep ts2phc"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Check <code>phc2sys</code> (system clock sync) status by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc logs -n openshift-ptp <linuxptp-daemon-pod> -c linuxptp-daemon-container | grep phc2sys"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>The output shows synchronization status messages for <code>phc2sys</code>.</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"phc2sys[xxx]: CLOCK_REALTIME phc offset -17 s2 freq -13865 delay 2305"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      </ol>
      <h3 id="configuring-gnss-to-ntp-failover-sno_configuring-ptp" class="group scroll-mt-20">Creating a PTP Grandmaster configuration with GNSS failover on Single Node OpenShift<a href="#configuring-gnss-to-ntp-failover-sno_configuring-ptp" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h3>
      <p class="_abstract">This procedure configures a T-GM (Telecom Grandmaster) clock on single-node OpenShift that uses an Intel E810 Westport Channel NIC as the PTP grandmaster clock with GNSS to NTP failover capabilities.</p>
      <p><strong>Prerequisites</strong></p>
      <ul>
      <li>For T-GM clocks in production environments, install an Intel E810 Westport Channel NIC in the bare metal single-node OpenShift host.</li>
      <li>Install the OpenShift CLI (<code>oc</code>).</li>
      <li>Log in as a user with <code>cluster-admin</code> privileges.</li>
      <li>Install the PTP Operator.</li>
      </ul>
      <p><strong>Procedure</strong></p>
      <ol>
      <li>
      Verify the PTP Operator installation by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc get pods -n openshift-ptp -o wide"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>The output is similar to the following listing the PTP Operator pod and the single <code>linuxptp-daemon</code> pod:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"NAME                            READY   STATUS    RESTARTS   AGE   IP              NODE                   NOMINATED NODE   READINESS GATES\nlinuxptp-daemon-xz8km           2/2     Running   0          15m   192.168.1.50    mysno-sno.demo.lab     <none>           <none>\nptp-operator-75c77dbf86-xm9kl   1/1     Running   0          20m   10.129.0.45     mysno-sno.demo.lab     <none>           <none>"} lang="terminal" />
      <ul>
      <li><code>ptp-operator-*</code>: The PTP Operator pod (one instance in the cluster).</li>
      <li><code>linuxptp-daemon-*</code>: The linuxptp daemon pod. On single-node OpenShift, there is only one daemon pod running on the master node. The daemon pod should show <code>2/2</code> in the READY column, indicating both containers (<code>linuxptp-daemon-container</code> and <code>kube-rbac-proxy</code>) are running.</li>
      </ul>
      </li>
      <li>
      Check which network interfaces support hardware timestamping by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc get NodePtpDevice -n openshift-ptp -o yaml"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>The output is similar to the following one, showing the NodePtpDevice resource for the single-node OpenShift node with PTP-capable network interfaces:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"apiVersion: v1\nitems:\n- apiVersion: ptp.openshift.io/v1\n  kind: NodePtpDevice\n  metadata:\n    name: mysno-sno.demo.lab\n    namespace: openshift-ptp\n  spec: {}\n  status:\n    devices:\n    - name: ens7f0\n      hwConfig:\n        phcIndex: 0\n    - name: ens7f1\n      hwConfig:\n        phcIndex: 1\nkind: List\nmetadata:\n  resourceVersion: \"\""} lang="yaml" />
      <p>In this example output:</p>
      <ul>
      <li><code>ens7f0</code> and <code>ens7f1</code> are PTP-capable interfaces (Intel E810 NIC ports).</li>
      <li>
      <code>phcIndex</code> indicates the PTP Hardware Clock number (maps to <code>/dev/ptp0</code>, <code>/dev/ptp1</code>, etc.)
      <Alert variant="note" class="my-4">
        <AlertTitle>Note</AlertTitle>
        <AlertDescription>
          <p>On single-node OpenShift clusters, you will see only one NodePtpDevice resource for the single master node.</p>
        </AlertDescription>
      </Alert>
      </li>
      </ul>
      </li>
      <li>
      The PTP profile uses node labels for matching. Check your machine config pool (MCP) to verify the master MCP by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc get mcp"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>The output is similar to the following:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"NAME     CONFIG                  UPDATED   UPDATING   DEGRADED   MACHINECOUNT   READYMACHINECOUNT   UPDATEDMACHINECOUNT DEGRADEDMACHINECOUNT AGE\nmaster   rendered-master-a1b1*   True      False      False      1              1                   1                   0                    45d\nworker   rendered-worker-f6e5*   True      False      False      0              0                   0                   0                    45d"} lang="terminal" />
      <Alert variant="note" class="my-4">
        <AlertTitle>Note</AlertTitle>
        <AlertDescription>
          <p>The CONFIG column shows a truncated hash of the rendered MachineConfig. In actual output, this will be a full 64-character hash like <code>rendered-master-a1b2c3d4e5f6a7b8c9d0e1f2a3b4c5d6</code>.</p>
        </AlertDescription>
      </Alert>
      <p>On single-node OpenShift clusters, the master MCP shows <code>MACHINECOUNT</code> of 1 (the single node), and the worker MCP shows <code>MACHINECOUNT</code> of 0. The PTP profile must target the <code>master</code> node label.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
      Create a <code>PtpConfig</code> custom resource (CR) that configures the T-GM clock with GNSS to NTP failover. Save the following YAML configuration to a file named <code>ptp-config-gnss-ntp-failover-sno.yaml</code>.
      <CodeBlock code={"# The grandmaster profile is provided for testing only\n# It is not installed on production clusters\napiVersion: ptp.openshift.io/v1\nkind: PtpConfig\nmetadata:\n  name: grandmaster\n  namespace: openshift-ptp\n  annotations:\n    ran.openshift.io/ztp-deploy-wave: \"10\"\nspec:\n  profile:\n  - name: \"grandmaster\"\n    ptp4lOpts: \"-2 --summary_interval -4\"\n    phc2sysOpts: -r -u 0 -m -N 8 -R 16 -s ens7f0 -n 24\n    ptpSchedulingPolicy: SCHED_FIFO\n    ptpSchedulingPriority: 10\n    ptpSettings:\n      logReduce: \"true\"\n\n    # --- FAILOVER CONFIGURATION ---\n    # Holdover time: 14400 seconds (4 hours) before switching to NTP\n    ts2phcOpts: \"--ts2phc.holdover 14400\"\n\n    # Configure Chronyd (Secondary Time Source)\n    chronydOpts: \"-d\"\n    chronydConf: |\n      server time.nist.gov iburst\n      makestep 1.0 -1\n      pidfile /var/run/chronyd.pid\n\n    plugins:\n      # E810 Hardware-Specific Configuration\n      e810:\n        enableDefaultConfig: false\n        settings:\n          LocalHoldoverTimeout: 14400\n          LocalMaxHoldoverOffSet: 1500\n          MaxInSpecOffset: 1500\n        pins:\n          # Syntax guide:\n          # - The 1st number in each pair must be one of:\n          #    0 - Disabled\n          #    1 - RX\n          #    2 - TX\n          # - The 2nd number in each pair must match the channel number\n          ens7f0:\n            SMA1: 0 1\n            SMA2: 0 2\n            U.FL1: 0 1\n            U.FL2: 0 2\n        ublxCmds:\n          - args: #ubxtool -P 29.20 -z CFG-HW-ANT_CFG_VOLTCTRL,1\n              - \"-P\"\n              - \"29.20\"\n              - \"-z\"\n              - \"CFG-HW-ANT_CFG_VOLTCTRL,1\"\n            reportOutput: false\n          - args: #ubxtool -P 29.20 -e GPS\n              - \"-P\"\n              - \"29.20\"\n              - \"-e\"\n              - \"GPS\"\n            reportOutput: false\n          - args: #ubxtool -P 29.20 -d Galileo\n              - \"-P\"\n              - \"29.20\"\n              - \"-d\"\n              - \"Galileo\"\n            reportOutput: false\n          - args: #ubxtool -P 29.20 -d GLONASS\n              - \"-P\"\n              - \"29.20\"\n              - \"-d\"\n              - \"GLONASS\"\n            reportOutput: false\n          - args: #ubxtool -P 29.20 -d BeiDou\n              - \"-P\"\n              - \"29.20\"\n              - \"-d\"\n              - \"BeiDou\"\n            reportOutput: false\n          - args: #ubxtool -P 29.20 -d SBAS\n              - \"-P\"\n              - \"29.20\"\n              - \"-d\"\n              - \"SBAS\"\n            reportOutput: false\n          - args: #ubxtool -P 29.20 -t -w 5 -v 1 -e SURVEYIN,600,50000\n              - \"-P\"\n              - \"29.20\"\n              - \"-t\"\n              - \"-w\"\n              - \"5\"\n              - \"-v\"\n              - \"1\"\n              - \"-e\"\n              - \"SURVEYIN,600,50000\"\n            reportOutput: true\n          - args: #ubxtool -P 29.20 -p MON-HW\n              - \"-P\"\n              - \"29.20\"\n              - \"-p\"\n              - \"MON-HW\"\n            reportOutput: true\n          - args: #ubxtool -P 29.20 -p CFG-MSG,1,38,248\n              - \"-P\"\n              - \"29.20\"\n              - \"-p\"\n              - \"CFG-MSG,1,38,248\"\n            reportOutput: true\n\n      # NTP Failover Plugin\n      ntpfailover:\n        gnssFailover: true\n\n    # --- GNSS (ts2phc) CONFIGURATION (Primary Source) ---\n    ts2phcConf: |\n      [nmea]\n      ts2phc.master 1\n      [global]\n      use_syslog  0\n      verbose 1\n      logging_level 7\n      ts2phc.pulsewidth 100000000\n      ts2phc.nmea_serialport /dev/ttyGNSS_1700_0\n      leapfile  /usr/share/zoneinfo/leap-seconds.list\n      [ens7f0]\n      ts2phc.extts_polarity rising\n      ts2phc.extts_correction 0\n\n    # --- PTP4L CONFIGURATION (Grandmaster Role) ---\n    ptp4lConf: |\n      [ens7f0]\n      masterOnly 1\n      [ens7f1]\n      masterOnly 1\n      [global]\n      #\n      # Default Data Set\n      #\n      twoStepFlag 1\n      priority1 128\n      priority2 128\n      domainNumber 24\n      #utc_offset 37\n      clockClass 6\n      clockAccuracy 0x27\n      offsetScaledLogVariance 0xFFFF\n      free_running 0\n      freq_est_interval 1\n      dscp_event 0\n      dscp_general 0\n      dataset_comparison G.8275.x\n      G.8275.defaultDS.localPriority 128\n      #\n      # Port Data Set\n      #\n      logAnnounceInterval -3\n      logSyncInterval -4\n      logMinDelayReqInterval -4\n      logMinPdelayReqInterval 0\n      announceReceiptTimeout 3\n      syncReceiptTimeout 0\n      delayAsymmetry 0\n      fault_reset_interval -4\n      neighborPropDelayThresh 20000000\n      masterOnly 0\n      G.8275.portDS.localPriority 128\n      #\n      # Run time options\n      #\n      assume_two_step 0\n      logging_level 6\n      path_trace_enabled 0\n      follow_up_info 0\n      hybrid_e2e 0\n      inhibit_multicast_service 0\n      net_sync_monitor 0\n      tc_spanning_tree 0\n      tx_timestamp_timeout 50\n      unicast_listen 0\n      unicast_master_table 0\n      unicast_req_duration 3600\n      use_syslog 1\n      verbose 0\n      summary_interval -4\n      kernel_leap 1\n      check_fup_sync 0\n      clock_class_threshold 7\n      #\n      # Servo Options\n      #\n      pi_proportional_const 0.0\n      pi_integral_const 0.0\n      pi_proportional_scale 0.0\n      pi_proportional_exponent -0.3\n      pi_proportional_norm_max 0.7\n      pi_integral_scale 0.0\n      pi_integral_exponent 0.4\n      pi_integral_norm_max 0.3\n      step_threshold 2.0\n      first_step_threshold 0.00002\n      clock_servo pi\n      sanity_freq_limit  200000000\n      ntpshm_segment 0\n      #\n      # Transport options\n      #\n      transportSpecific 0x0\n      ptp_dst_mac 01:1B:19:00:00:00\n      p2p_dst_mac 01:80:C2:00:00:0E\n      udp_ttl 1\n      udp6_scope 0x0E\n      uds_address /var/run/ptp4l\n      #\n      # Default interface options\n      #\n      clock_type BC\n      network_transport L2\n      delay_mechanism E2E\n      time_stamping hardware\n      tsproc_mode filter\n      delay_filter moving_median\n      delay_filter_length 10\n      egressLatency 0\n      ingressLatency 0\n      boundary_clock_jbod 0\n      #\n      # Clock description\n      #\n      productDescription ;;\n      revisionData ;;\n      manufacturerIdentity 00:00:00\n      userDescription ;\n      timeSource 0x20\n    ptpClockThreshold:\n      holdOverTimeout: 5\n      maxOffsetThreshold: 100\n      minOffsetThreshold: -100\n  recommend:\n  - profile: \"grandmaster\"\n    priority: 4\n    match:\n    - nodeLabel: node-role.kubernetes.io/master"} lang="yaml" />
      <Alert variant="info" class="my-4">
        <AlertTitle>Important</AlertTitle>
        <AlertDescription>
          <p>Replace the example interface names (<code>ens7f0</code>, <code>ens7f1</code>) with your actual E810 NIC interface names found in step 2. Common E810 interface naming patterns include <code>ens7f0</code>, <code>ens8f0</code>, <code>eth0</code>, <code>enp2s0f0</code>, and so on. The exact name depends on your system BIOS settings and Linux network device naming conventions. Also, replace <code>/dev/ttyGNSS_1700_0</code> with your actual GNSS serial port device path. The <code>nodeLabel</code> is set to <code>node-role.kubernetes.io/master</code> to target the single-node OpenShift master node which serves all roles.</p>
        </AlertDescription>
      </Alert>
      <p>The configuration includes the following components:</p>
      <ul>
      <li>
      <strong>PTP4L options</strong>:
      <ul>
      <li><code>-2</code>: Use PTP version 2</li>
      <li><code>--summary_interval -4</code>: Log summary every 2^(-4) = 0.0625 seconds</li>
      </ul>
      </li>
      <li>
      <strong>PHC2SYS options:</strong>
      <ul>
      <li><code>-r</code>: Synchronize system clock from PTP hardware clock</li>
      <li><code>-u 0</code>: Update rate multiplier</li>
      <li><code>-m</code>: Print messages to stdout</li>
      <li><code>-N 8</code>: Domain number for ptp4l</li>
      <li><code>-R 16</code>: Update rate</li>
      <li><code>-s ens7f0</code>: Source interface (replace with your E810 interface name)</li>
      <li><code>-n 24</code>: Domain number</li>
      </ul>
      </li>
      <li>
      <strong>Failover configuration:</strong>
      <ul>
      <li><code>ts2phcOpts --ts2phc.holdover 14400</code>: 4-hour holdover before switching to NTP</li>
      <li><code>chronydConf</code>: NTP server configuration for failover replace <code>time.nist.gov</code> with your preferred NTP server</li>
      <li><code>ntpfailover plugin</code>: Enables automatic GNSS-to-NTP switching with <code>gnssFailover: true</code>.</li>
      </ul>
      </li>
      <li>
      <strong>E810 plugin configuration:</strong>
      <ul>
      <li><code>LocalHoldoverTimeout: 14400</code>: E810 hardware holdover timeout (4 hours)</li>
      <li><code>pins</code>: Configuration for 1PPS input on E810 physical pins (U.FL2, SMA1, SMA2, U.FL1)</li>
      <li><code>ublxCmds</code>: Commands to configure u-blox GNSS receiver (enable GPS, disable other constellations, set survey-in mode)</li>
      </ul>
      </li>
      <li>
      <strong>GNSS (ts2phc) configuration:</strong>
      <ul>
      <li><code>ts2phc.nmea_serialport /dev/ttyGNSS_1700_0</code>: GNSS serial port device path (replace with your actual GNSS device)</li>
      <li><code>ts2phc.extts_polarity rising</code>: 1PPS signal on rising edge</li>
      <li><code>ts2phc.pulsewidth 100000000</code>: 1PPS pulse width in nanoseconds</li>
      </ul>
      </li>
      <li>
      <strong>PTP4L configuration:</strong>
      <ul>
      <li><code>masterOnly 1</code>: Interface acts only as PTP master</li>
      <li><code>clockClass 6</code>: GPS-synchronized quality level</li>
      <li><code>domainNumber 24</code>: PTP domain</li>
      <li><code>clock_type BC</code>: Boundary Clock mode</li>
      <li><code>time_stamping hardware</code>: Use hardware timestamps from E810 NIC</li>
      </ul>
      </li>
      </ul>
      </li>
      <li>
      Apply the <code>PtpConfig</code> CR by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc apply -f ptp-config-gnss-ntp-failover-sno.yaml"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>The output is similar to the following:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"ptpconfig.ptp.openshift.io/grandmaster created"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      </ol>
      <p><strong>Verification</strong></p>
      <ol>
      <li>
      The PTP daemon checks for profile updates every 30 seconds. Wait approximately 30 seconds, then verify by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc get ptpconfig -n openshift-ptp"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>The output is similar to the following:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"NAME           AGE\ngrandmaster    2m"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Check the NodePtpDevice to see if the profile is applied. First, get your single-node OpenShift node name:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc get nodes"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>The output is similar to the following:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"NAME                 STATUS   ROLES                         AGE     VERSION\nmysno-sno.demo.lab   Ready    control-plane,master,worker   4h19m   v1.35.4"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>Then describe the NodePtpDevice using your node name:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc describe nodeptpdevice mysno-sno.demo.lab -n openshift-ptp"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Check if the profile is being loaded by monitoring the daemon logs. First, get the daemon pod name:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc get pods -n openshift-ptp | grep linuxptp-daemon"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>The output shows the single linuxptp-daemon pod:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"linuxptp-daemon-xz8km           2/2     Running   0          15m"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>Then check the logs using the pod name:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc logs -n openshift-ptp linuxptp-daemon-xz8km -c linuxptp-daemon-container --tail=100"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>Success indicators in the logs are:</p>
      <ul>
      <li><code>load profiles</code> - Profile is being loaded</li>
      <li><code>in applyNodePTPProfiles</code> - Profile is being applied</li>
      <li>No <code>ptp profile doesn't exist for node</code> errors</li>
      </ul>
      </li>
      <li>
      Check <code>chronyd</code> status to verify NTP is running as the secondary time source by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc logs -n openshift-ptp linuxptp-daemon-xz8km -c linuxptp-daemon-container | grep chronyd"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>The output is similar to the following:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"chronyd version 4.5 starting\nAdded source ID#0000000001 (time.nist.gov)"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Check GNSS/gpsd by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc logs -n openshift-ptp linuxptp-daemon-xz8km -c linuxptp-daemon-container | grep gpsd"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>The output shows the following when GNSS is functioning correctly:</p>
      <ul>
      <li><code>gpsd</code> starting successfully</li>
      <li>No <code>No such file or directory</code> errors exist</li>
      </ul>
      </li>
      <li>
      Check <code>ts2phc</code> (GNSS synchronization) status by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc logs -n openshift-ptp linuxptp-daemon-xz8km -c linuxptp-daemon-container | grep ts2phc"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Check <code>phc2sys</code> (system clock sync) status by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc logs -n openshift-ptp linuxptp-daemon-xz8km -c linuxptp-daemon-container | grep phc2sys"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>The output shows synchronization status messages for <code>phc2sys</code>.</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"phc2sys[xxx]: CLOCK_REALTIME phc offset -17 s2 freq -13865 delay 2305"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      </ol>
      <h2 id="cnf-troubleshooting-common-ptp-operator-issues_configuring-ptp" class="group scroll-mt-20">Troubleshooting common PTP Operator issues<a href="#cnf-troubleshooting-common-ptp-operator-issues_configuring-ptp" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h2>
      <p class="_abstract">Troubleshoot common problems with the PTP Operator by performing the following steps.</p>
      <p><strong>Prerequisites</strong></p>
      <ul>
      <li>Install the OpenShift Container Platform CLI (<code>oc</code>).</li>
      <li>Log in as a user with <code>cluster-admin</code> privileges.</li>
      <li>Install the PTP Operator on a bare-metal cluster with hosts that support PTP.</li>
      </ul>
      <p><strong>Procedure</strong></p>
      <ol>
      <li>
      Check the Operator and operands are successfully deployed in the cluster for the configured nodes.
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc get pods -n openshift-ptp -o wide"} lang="terminal" />
      <CodeBlock code={"NAME                            READY   STATUS    RESTARTS   AGE     IP            NODE\nlinuxptp-daemon-lmvgn           3/3     Running   0          4d17h   10.1.196.24   compute-0.example.com\nlinuxptp-daemon-qhfg7           3/3     Running   0          4d17h   10.1.196.25   compute-1.example.com\nptp-operator-6b8dcbf7f4-zndk7   1/1     Running   0          5d7h    10.129.0.61   control-plane-1.example.com"} lang="terminal" title="Example output" />
      <Alert variant="note" class="my-4">
        <AlertTitle>Note</AlertTitle>
        <AlertDescription>
          <p>When the PTP fast event bus is enabled, the number of ready <code>linuxptp-daemon</code> pods is <code>3/3</code>. If the PTP fast event bus is not enabled, <code>2/2</code> is displayed.</p>
        </AlertDescription>
      </Alert>
      </li>
      <li>
      Check that supported hardware is found in the cluster.
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc -n openshift-ptp get nodeptpdevices.ptp.openshift.io"} lang="terminal" />
      <CodeBlock code={"NAME                                  AGE\ncontrol-plane-0.example.com           10d\ncontrol-plane-1.example.com           10d\ncompute-0.example.com                 10d\ncompute-1.example.com                 10d\ncompute-2.example.com                 10d"} lang="terminal" title="Example output" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Check the available PTP network interfaces for a node:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc -n openshift-ptp get nodeptpdevices.ptp.openshift.io <node_name> -o yaml"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>where:</p>
      <dl class="my-4 space-y-2">
      <dt class="font-semibold">&lt;node_name&gt;</dt>
      <dd class="ml-6 text-muted-foreground"><p>Specifies the node you want to query, for example, <code>compute-0.example.com</code>.</p></dd>
      </dl>
      <CodeBlock code={"apiVersion: ptp.openshift.io/v1\nkind: NodePtpDevice\nmetadata:\n  creationTimestamp: \"2021-09-14T16:52:33Z\"\n  generation: 1\n  name: compute-0.example.com\n  namespace: openshift-ptp\n  resourceVersion: \"177400\"\n  uid: 30413db0-4d8d-46da-9bef-737bacd548fd\nspec: {}\nstatus:\n  devices:\n  - name: eno1\n  - name: eno2\n  - name: eno3\n  - name: eno4\n  - name: enp5s0f0\n  - name: enp5s0f1"} lang="yaml" title="Example output" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Check that the PTP interface is successfully synchronized to the primary clock by accessing the <code>linuxptp-daemon</code> pod for the corresponding node.
      <ol>
      <li>
      Get the name of the <code>linuxptp-daemon</code> pod and corresponding node you want to troubleshoot by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc get pods -n openshift-ptp -o wide"} lang="terminal" />
      <CodeBlock code={"NAME                            READY   STATUS    RESTARTS   AGE     IP            NODE\nlinuxptp-daemon-lmvgn           3/3     Running   0          4d17h   10.1.196.24   compute-0.example.com\nlinuxptp-daemon-qhfg7           3/3     Running   0          4d17h   10.1.196.25   compute-1.example.com\nptp-operator-6b8dcbf7f4-zndk7   1/1     Running   0          5d7h    10.129.0.61   control-plane-1.example.com"} lang="terminal" title="Example output" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Remote shell into the required <code>linuxptp-daemon</code> container:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc rsh -n openshift-ptp -c linuxptp-daemon-container <linux_daemon_container>"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>where:</p>
      <dl class="my-4 space-y-2">
      <dt class="font-semibold">&lt;linux_daemon_container&gt;</dt>
      <dd class="ml-6 text-muted-foreground"><p>is the container you want to diagnose, for example <code>linuxptp-daemon-lmvgn</code>.</p></dd>
      </dl>
      </li>
      <li>
      In the remote shell connection to the <code>linuxptp-daemon</code> container, use the PTP Management Client (<code>pmc</code>) tool to diagnose the network interface. Run the following <code>pmc</code> command to check the sync status of the PTP device, for example <code>ptp4l</code>.
      <CodeBlock code={"# pmc -u -f /var/run/ptp4l.0.config -b 0 'GET PORT_DATA_SET'"} lang="terminal" />
      <CodeBlock code={"sending: GET PORT_DATA_SET\n    40a6b7.fffe.166ef0-1 seq 0 RESPONSE MANAGEMENT PORT_DATA_SET\n        portIdentity            40a6b7.fffe.166ef0-1\n        portState               SLAVE\n        logMinDelayReqInterval  -4\n        peerMeanPathDelay       0\n        logAnnounceInterval     -3\n        announceReceiptTimeout  3\n        logSyncInterval         -4\n        delayMechanism          1\n        logMinPdelayReqInterval -4\n        versionNumber           2"} lang="terminal" title="Example output when the node is successfully synced to the primary clock" />
      </li>
      </ol>
      </li>
      <li>
      For GNSS-sourced grandmaster clocks, verify that the in-tree NIC ice driver is correct by running the following command, for example:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc rsh -n openshift-ptp -c linuxptp-daemon-container linuxptp-daemon-74m2g ethtool -i ens7f0"} lang="terminal" />
      <CodeBlock code={"driver: ice\nversion: 5.14.0-356.bz2232515.el9.x86_64\nfirmware-version: 4.22 0x8001778b 1.3346.0"} lang="terminal" title="Example output" />
      </li>
      <li>
      For GNSS-sourced grandmaster clocks, verify that the <code>linuxptp-daemon</code> container is receiving signal from the GNSS antenna. If the container is not receiving the GNSS signal, the <code>/dev/gnss0</code> file is not populated. To verify, run the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc rsh -n openshift-ptp -c linuxptp-daemon-container linuxptp-daemon-jnz6r cat /dev/gnss0"} lang="terminal" />
      <CodeBlock code={"$GNRMC,125223.00,A,4233.24463,N,07126.64561,W,0.000,,300823,,,A,V*0A\n$GNVTG,,T,,M,0.000,N,0.000,K,A*3D\n$GNGGA,125223.00,4233.24463,N,07126.64561,W,1,12,99.99,98.6,M,-33.1,M,,*7E\n$GNGSA,A,3,25,17,19,11,12,06,05,04,09,20,,,99.99,99.99,99.99,1*37\n$GPGSV,3,1,10,04,12,039,41,05,31,222,46,06,50,064,48,09,28,064,42,1*62"} lang="terminal" title="Example output" />
      </li>
      </ol>
      <h2 id="cnf-getting-the-dpll-firmware-version-for-intel-800-series-nics_configuring-ptp" class="group scroll-mt-20">Getting the DPLL firmware version for the CGU in an Intel 800 series NIC<a href="#cnf-getting-the-dpll-firmware-version-for-intel-800-series-nics_configuring-ptp" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h2>
      <p class="_abstract">You can get the digital phase-locked loop (DPLL) firmware version for the Clock Generation Unit (CGU) in an Intel 800 series NIC by opening a debug shell to the cluster node and querying the NIC hardware.</p>
      <p><strong>Prerequisites</strong></p>
      <ul>
      <li>You have installed the OpenShift CLI (<code>oc</code>).</li>
      <li>You have logged in as a user with <code>cluster-admin</code> privileges.</li>
      <li>You have installed an Intel 800 series NIC in the cluster host.</li>
      <li>You have installed the PTP Operator on a bare-metal cluster with hosts that support PTP.</li>
      </ul>
      <p><strong>Procedure</strong></p>
      <ol>
      <li>
      Start a debug pod by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc debug node/<node_name>"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>where:</p>
      <dl class="my-4 space-y-2">
      <dt class="font-semibold">&lt;node_name&gt;</dt>
      <dd class="ml-6 text-muted-foreground"><p>Is the node where you have installed the Intel 800 series NIC.</p></dd>
      </dl>
      </li>
      <li>
      Check the CGU firmware version in the NIC by using the <code>devlink</code> tool and the bus and device name where the NIC is installed. For example, run the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"sh-4.4# devlink dev info <bus_name>/<device_name> | grep cgu"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>where:</p>
      <dl class="my-4 space-y-2">
      <dt class="font-semibold">&lt;bus_name&gt;</dt>
      <dd class="ml-6 text-muted-foreground"><p>Is the bus where the NIC is installed. For example, <code>pci</code>.</p></dd>
      <dt class="font-semibold">&lt;device_name&gt;</dt>
      <dd class="ml-6 text-muted-foreground"><p>Is the NIC device name. For example, <code>0000:51:00.0</code>.</p></dd>
      </dl>
      <CodeBlock code={"cgu.id 36\nfw.cgu 8032.16973825.6021"} lang="terminal" title="Example output" />
      <p>where:</p>
      <dl class="my-4 space-y-2">
      <dt class="font-semibold"><code>cgu.id 36</code></dt>
      <dd class="ml-6 text-muted-foreground"><p>CGU hardware revision number.</p></dd>
      <dt class="font-semibold"><code>fw.cgu 8032.16973825.6021</code></dt>
      <dd class="ml-6 text-muted-foreground"><p>DPLL firmware version running in the CGU, where the DPLL firmware version is <code>6201</code>, and the DPLL model is <code>8032</code>. The string <code>16973825</code> is a shorthand representation of the binary version of the DPLL firmware version (<code>1.3.0.1</code>).</p></dd>
      </dl>
      <Alert variant="note" class="my-4">
        <AlertTitle>Note</AlertTitle>
        <AlertDescription>
          <p>The firmware version has a leading nibble and 3 octets for each part of the version number. The number <code>16973825</code> in binary is <code>0001 0000 0011 0000 0000 0000 0001</code>. Use the binary value to decode the firmware version. For example:</p>
          <p><strong>DPLL firmware version</strong></p>
          <Table class="my-4">
          <TableHeader>
          <TableRow>
          <TableHead>Binary part</TableHead>
          <TableHead>Decimal value</TableHead>
          </TableRow>
          </TableHeader>
          <TableBody>
          <TableRow>
          <TableCell><code>0001</code></TableCell>
          <TableCell>1</TableCell>
          </TableRow>
          <TableRow>
          <TableCell><code>0000 0011</code></TableCell>
          <TableCell>3</TableCell>
          </TableRow>
          <TableRow>
          <TableCell><code>0000 0000</code></TableCell>
          <TableCell>0</TableCell>
          </TableRow>
          <TableRow>
          <TableCell><code>0000 0001</code></TableCell>
          <TableCell>1</TableCell>
          </TableRow>
          </TableBody>
          </Table>
        </AlertDescription>
      </Alert>
      </li>
      </ol>
      <h2 id="cnf-about-collecting-nro-data_configuring-ptp" class="group scroll-mt-20">Collecting PTP Operator data<a href="#cnf-about-collecting-nro-data_configuring-ptp" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h2>
      <p>You can use the <code>oc adm must-gather</code> command to collect information about your cluster, including features and objects associated with PTP Operator.</p>
      <p><strong>Prerequisites</strong></p>
      <ul>
      <li>You have access to the cluster as a user with the <code>cluster-admin</code> role.</li>
      <li>You have installed the OpenShift CLI (<code>oc</code>).</li>
      <li>You have installed the PTP Operator.</li>
      </ul>
      <p><strong>Procedure</strong></p>
      <ul>
      <li>
      To collect PTP Operator data with <code>must-gather</code>, you must specify the PTP Operator <code>must-gather</code> image.
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc adm must-gather --image=registry.redhat.io/openshift4/ptp-must-gather-rhel9:v4.22"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      </ul>
